  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "text": "A temporary code name became the permanent name and logo of a global wireless standard.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "consequence"
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "text": "The governing physics: The logo is a bind rune that merges the Younger Futhark runes for Harald Bluetooth's initials.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "mechanism"
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "text": "Bluetooth sounds like a technical acronym, but it is a king's nickname that was meant to be a placeholder.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "misconception"
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "text": "The Bluetooth logo is a Viking king's initials.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "text": "Two runes, merged into one symbol.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "text": "The name honours King Harald Bluetooth, who united Denmark and Norway.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "text": "At an industry meeting, Intel's Jim Kardach suggested it as a temporary code name.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "text": "Harald united Scandinavia. This radio link would unite PCs and phones.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C9",
      "text": "It was only a placeholder. RadioWire or PAN was meant to replace it.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "text": "PAN was already everywhere online, and RadioWire's trademark check ran out of time.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "text": "So the logo merges Harald's initials in runes: H and B.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "text": "King Harald Bluetooth Gormsson was known for uniting Denmark and Norway in 958.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "origin",
      "url": "https://www.bluetooth.com/about-us/bluetooth-origin/"
    },
    {
      "id": "C13",
      "text": "Harald's nickname came from a dead tooth of a dark blue-grey colour.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "context",
      "url": "https://www.bluetooth.com/about-us/bluetooth-origin/"
    },
    {
      "id": "C14",
      "text": "In 1996 Intel, Ericsson and Nokia met to plan the standardization of a short-range radio technology.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "context",
      "url": "https://www.bluetooth.com/about-us/bluetooth-origin/"
    },
    {
      "id": "C15",
      "text": "At that meeting Jim Kardach of Intel suggested Bluetooth as a temporary code name.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "origin",
      "url": "https://www.bluetooth.com/about-us/bluetooth-origin/"
    },
    {
      "id": "C16",
      "text": "Kardach compared Harald uniting Scandinavia with uniting the PC and cellular industries through a short-range wireless link.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "explanation",
      "url": "https://www.bluetooth.com/about-us/bluetooth-origin/"
    },
    {
      "id": "C17",
      "text": "Bluetooth was intended only as a placeholder name.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "surprising-detail",
      "url": "https://www.bluetooth.com/about-us/bluetooth-origin/"
    },
    {
      "id": "C18",
      "text": "The planned replacement names were RadioWire or PAN (Personal Area Networking).",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "surprising-detail",
      "url": "https://www.bluetooth.com/about-us/bluetooth-origin/"
    },
    {
      "id": "C19",
      "text": "PAN already had tens of thousands of hits on the internet.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "problem",
      "url": "https://www.bluetooth.com/about-us/bluetooth-origin/"
    },
    {
      "id": "C20",
      "text": "A full trademark search on RadioWire could not be completed in time for launch.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "problem",
      "url": "https://www.bluetooth.com/about-us/bluetooth-origin/"
    },
    {
      "id": "C21",
      "text": "The Bluetooth logo is a bind rune merging the Younger Futhark runes Hagall and Bjarkan, Harald's initials.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Bluetooth SIG — Origin of the Name",
      "role": "payoff",
      "url": "https://www.bluetooth.com/about-us/bluetooth-origin/"
    },
    {
      "id": "C22",
      "text": "Kardach proposed the code name after seeing a picture of Harald Bluetooth's runestone in a history of the Vikings.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Bluetooth",
      "role": "context",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bluetooth"
    },
    {
      "id": "C23",
      "text": "A symbol is a mark, sign, or word that indicates, signifies, or is understood as representing an idea, object, relationship, or mathematical formula.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "consequence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C24",
      "text": "Symbols allow people to go beyond what is known or seen by creating linkages between otherwise different concepts and experiences.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C25",
      "text": "All communication is achieved through the use of symbols: for example, a red octagon is a common symbol for \"STOP\"; on maps, blue lines often represent rivers; and a red rose often symbolizes love and compassion.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C26",
      "text": "Numerals are symbols for numbers; letters of an alphabet may be symbols for certain phonemes; and personal names are symbols representing individuals.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C27",
      "text": "The academic study of symbols is called semiotics.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C28",
      "text": "In the arts, symbolism is the use of a concrete element to represent a more abstract idea.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C29",
      "text": "In cartography, an organized collection of symbols forms a legend for a map.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C30",
      "text": "A literary or artistic symbol as an \"outward sign\" of something else is a metaphorical extension of this notion of a message from a sender to a recipient.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Etymology",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C31",
      "text": "In English, the meaning \"something which stands for something else\" was first recorded in 1590, in Edmund Spenser's Faerie Queene.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Etymology",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C32",
      "text": "Symbols are a means of complex communication that often can have multiple levels of meaning.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C33",
      "text": "Symbols are the basis of all human understanding and serve as vehicles of conception for all human knowledge.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C34",
      "text": "Symbols facilitate understanding of the world in which we live, thus serving as the grounds upon which we make judgments.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C35",
      "text": "In this way, people use symbols not only to make sense of the world around them but also to identify and cooperate in society through constitutive rhetoric.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C36",
      "text": "Human cultures use symbols to express specific ideologies and social structures and to represent aspects of their specific culture.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C37",
      "text": "Thus, symbols carry meanings that depend upon one's cultural background.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C38",
      "text": "As a result, the meaning of a symbol is not inherent in the symbol itself but is culturally learned.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C39",
      "text": "Heinrich Zimmer gives a concise overview of the nature, and perennial relevance, of symbols.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C40",
      "text": "Concepts and words are symbols, just as visions, rituals, and images are; so too are the manners and customs of daily life.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C41",
      "text": "Through all of these, a transcendent reality is mirrored.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C42",
      "text": "There are so many metaphors reflecting and implying something which, though thus variously expressed, is ineffable, though thus rendered multiform, remains inscrutable.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C43",
      "text": "Symbols hold the mind to truth but are not themselves the truth, hence it is delusory to borrow them.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C44",
      "text": "Each civilisation, every age, must bring forth its own.\" In the book Signs and Symbols, it is stated that A symbol ... is a visual image or sign representing an idea – a deeper indicator of universal truth.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Concepts and definitions",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C45",
      "text": "Semiotics is the study of signs, symbols, and signification as communicative behavior.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols and semiotics",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C46",
      "text": "Semiotics studies focus on the relationship of the signifier and the signified, also taking into account the interpretation of visual cues, body language, sound, and other contextual clues.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols and semiotics",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C47",
      "text": "Semiotics is linked with linguistics and psychology.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols and semiotics",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C48",
      "text": "Semioticians not only study what a symbol implies but also how it got its meaning and how it functions to make meaning in society.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols and semiotics",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C49",
      "text": "For example, symbols can cause confusion in translation when the same symbol means different things in the source and target languages.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols and semiotics",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C50",
      "text": "A potential error documented in survey translation is the symbol of \"x\" used to denote \"yes\" when marking a response in the English language surveys, but \"x\" usually means \"no\" in the Chinese convention.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols and semiotics",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C51",
      "text": "Symbols allow the human brain continuously to create meaning using sensory input and decode symbols through both denotation and connotation.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols and semiotics",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C52",
      "text": "An alternative definition of symbol, distinguishing it from the term sign was proposed by Swiss psychoanalyst Carl Jung.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C53",
      "text": "In his studies on what is now called Jungian archetypes, a sign stands for something known, as a word stands for its referent.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C54",
      "text": "He contrasted a sign with a symbol: something that is unknown and that cannot be made clear or precise.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C55",
      "text": "An example of a symbol in this sense is Christ as a symbol of the archetype called self.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C56",
      "text": "Kenneth Burke described Homo sapiens as a \"symbol-using, symbol making, and symbol misusing animal\" to suggest that a person creates symbols as well as misuses them.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C57",
      "text": "One example he uses to indicate what he means by the misuse of the symbol is the story of a man who, when told that a particular food item was whale blubber, could barely keep from throwing it up.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C58",
      "text": "Later, his friend discovered it was actually just a dumpling.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C59",
      "text": "But the man's reaction was a direct consequence of the symbol of \"blubber\" representing something inedible in his mind.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C60",
      "text": "In addition, the symbol of \"blubber\" was created by the man through various kinds of learning.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C61",
      "text": "Burke goes on to describe symbols as also being derived from Sigmund Freud's work on condensation and displacement, further stating that symbols are not just relevant to the theory of dreams but also to \"normal symbol systems\".",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C62",
      "text": "He says they are related through \"substitution\", where one word, phrase, or symbol is substituted for another in order to change the meaning.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C63",
      "text": "In other words, if one person does not understand a certain word or phrase, another person may substitute a synonym or symbol in order to get the meaning across.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C64",
      "text": "However, upon learning the new way of interpreting a specific symbol, the person may change his or her already-formed ideas to incorporate the new information.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C65",
      "text": "Jean Dalby Clift says that people not only add their own interpretations to symbols, but they also create personal symbols that represent their own understanding of their lives: what she calls \"core images\" of the person.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C66",
      "text": "Clift argues that symbolic work with these personal symbols or core images can be as useful as working with dream symbols in psychoanalysis or counseling.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C67",
      "text": "William Indick suggests that the symbols that are commonly found in myth, legend, and fantasy fulfill psychological functions and hence are why archetypes such as \"the hero\", \"the princess\" and \"the witch\" have remained popular for centuries.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "cause",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Psychoanalysis, rhetoric, and archetypes",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C68",
      "text": "Symbols can carry symbolic value in three primary forms: Ideological, comparative, and isomorphic.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbolic value",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C69",
      "text": "Ideological symbols such as religious and state symbols convey complex sets of beliefs and ideas that indicate \"the right thing to do\".",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbolic value",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C70",
      "text": "Comparative symbols such as prestigious office addresses, fine art, and prominent awards indicate answers to questions of \"better or worse\" and \"superior or inferior\".",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbolic value",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C71",
      "text": "Isomorphic symbols blend in with the surrounding cultural environment such that they enable individuals and organizations to conform to their surroundings and evade social and political scrutiny.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbolic value",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C72",
      "text": "Examples of symbols with isomorphic value include wearing a professional dress during business meetings, shaking hands to greet others in the West, or bowing to greet others in the East.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbolic value",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C73",
      "text": "A single symbol can carry multiple distinct meanings such that it provides multiple types of symbolic value.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbolic value",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C74",
      "text": "Paul Tillich argued that, while signs are invented and forgotten, symbols are born and die.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Paul Tillich",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C75",
      "text": "There are, therefore, dead and living symbols.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Paul Tillich",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C76",
      "text": "A living symbol can reveal to an individual hidden levels of meaning and transcendent or religious realities.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Paul Tillich",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C77",
      "text": "For Tillich a symbol always \"points beyond itself\" to something that is unquantifiable and mysterious; symbols open up the \"depth dimension of reality itself\".",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Paul Tillich",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C78",
      "text": "Symbols are complex, and their meanings can evolve as the individual or culture evolves.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Paul Tillich",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C79",
      "text": "When a symbol loses its meaning and power for an individual or culture, it becomes a dead symbol.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Paul Tillich",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C80",
      "text": "When a symbol becomes identified with the deeper reality to which it refers, it becomes idolatrous as the \"symbol is taken for reality.\" The symbol itself is substituted for the deeper meaning it intends to convey.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Paul Tillich",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C81",
      "text": "The unique nature of a symbol is that it gives access to deeper layers of reality that are otherwise inaccessible.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Paul Tillich",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C82",
      "text": "A symbol's meaning may be modified by various factors including popular usage, history, and contextual intent.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Role of context in symbolism",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C83",
      "text": "The history of a symbol is one of many factors in determining a particular symbol's apparent meaning.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Historical meaning",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C84",
      "text": "Consequently, symbols with emotive power carry problems analogous to false etymologies.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Historical meaning",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C85",
      "text": "The context of a symbol may change its meaning.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Context",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C86",
      "text": "Similar five-pointed stars might signify a law enforcement officer or a member of the armed services, depending upon the uniform.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Context",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C87",
      "text": "Symbols are used in cartography to communicate geographical information (generally as point, line, or area features).",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols in cartography",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C88",
      "text": "As with other symbols, visual variables such as size, shape, orientation, texture, and pattern provide meaning to the symbol.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols in cartography",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C89",
      "text": "According to semiotics, map symbols are \"read\" by map users when they make a connection between the graphic mark on the map (the sign), a general concept (the interpretant), and a particular feature of the real world (the referent).",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols in cartography",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C90",
      "text": "Map symbols can thus be categorized by how they suggest this connection: Pictorial symbols (also \"image\", \"iconic\", or \"replicative\") appears as the real-world feature, although it is often in a generalized manner; e.g. a tree icon to represents a forest or green denoting vegetation.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols in cartography",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C91",
      "text": "Functional symbols (also \"representational\") directly represent the activity that takes place at the represented feature; e.g. a picture of a skier to represent a ski resort or a tent to represent a campground.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols in cartography",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C92",
      "text": "Conceptual symbols directly represent a concept related to the represented feature; e.g. a dollar sign to represent an ATM, or a Star of David to represent a Jewish synagogue.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols in cartography",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C93",
      "text": "Conventional symbols (also \"associative\") do not have any intuitive relationship but are so commonly used that map readers eventually learn to recognize them; e.g. a red line to represent a highway or a cross to represent a hospital.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols in cartography",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C94",
      "text": "Abstract/geometric symbols (also \"ad hoc\") are arbitrary shapes chosen by the cartographer to represent a certain feature.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Symbols in cartography",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C95",
      "text": "A symbolic action is an action that symbolizes or signals what the actor wants or believes.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Related terms",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C96",
      "text": "The action conveys meaning to the viewers.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Related terms",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C97",
      "text": "Symbolic action may overlap with symbolic speech, such as the use of flag burning to express hostility or saluting the flag to express patriotism.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Related terms",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C98",
      "text": "In response to intense public criticism, businesses, organizations, and governments may take symbolic actions rather than, or in addition to, directly addressing the identified problems.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Symbol",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol",
      "section": "Related terms",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    }
  ],
